# This workspace holds two React majors on purpose, and npm needs telling that it is deliberate.
#
# The web app is on React 19; React Native 0.76 declares a peer dependency on React 18. Both are
# correct for their own package, and npm's strict peer resolver cannot satisfy the combined graph,
# so a plain `npm ci` fails outright on a clean checkout with ERESOLVE.
#
# The install it refuses to perform is the right one. The lockfile already pins React 19.2.7 at the
# root, where the frontend picks it up, and a nested React 18.3.1 under packages/mobile, which is
# what Metro resolves for the app. Nothing ends up running against the wrong major; npm simply will
# not choose that arrangement unless the strict check is relaxed.
#
# This lives here rather than as a flag on each command because it was previously a flag on *some*
# of them: both Dockerfiles passed --legacy-peer-deps and nothing else did, so the images built and
# a fresh clone did not — and CI, added later, failed at `npm ci` before running a single test. One
# file now decides it for every install path: a developer's clone, CI, and the production images.
legacy-peer-deps=true
